{%- doc -%}
Styling shared by every "zoomable media" item rendered via snippets/media-zoom-slide.liquid: the
video sound/play-pause/product controls (position, hover, touch relocation next to a progress bar)
and the zoom-to-lightbox expand/collapse look. This is the Carousel block's own <style> block
(sections/media-blocks.liquid) generalized so it can be shared with any other caller that renders
zoomable media items (eg. FAQ's "Text + image/video" block).

Must be rendered inside a <style> tag by the caller, once per instance (wrapper_id/lightbox_id pair).

@param {string} wrapper_id - id of the ancestor element wrapping every media item and the lightbox (same id passed to snippets/media-zoom-script.liquid). Also used to scope the lightbox-expanded positioning rules - video-media/img stay descendants of this wrapper in the DOM even once they visually escape to position: fixed, so scoping to it (rather than needing the enclosing section's id) is enough and avoids an extra id having to be threaded through every caller.
@param {string} lightbox_id - id of the media-lightbox element
@param {string} [corner_radius] - CSS value matching the media item's own border-radius (eg. "var(--media-blocks-corner-radius)" or "12px"), reused so the expanded lightbox media keeps the same rounded corners
@param {string} [controls_position] - 'top' (default) or 'progress_bar'
@param {string} [desktop_controls_position] - 'top_right' (default) or 'center', only used when controls_position is 'progress_bar'
@param {string} [controls_slot_id] - id of the element controls are relocated into on touch devices, required when controls_position is 'progress_bar'
@param {boolean} [has_multiple_slides] - whether there is an actual progress bar to reorder/hide the product link next to (carousel-only)
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- liquid
  assign resolved_corner_radius = corner_radius | default: '0px'
  assign resolved_controls_position = controls_position | default: 'top'
  assign resolved_desktop_controls_position = desktop_controls_position | default: 'top_right'
-%}

#{{ wrapper_id }} video-media > img,
#{{ wrapper_id }} video-media > video {
  opacity: 1 !important;
}

{%- comment -%}
Safari/WebKit doesn't reliably clip a <video> element to an ANCESTOR's overflow: hidden +
border-radius (a long-standing WebKit bug) - .content-box (media-zoom-slide.liquid) sets both
inline, which clips the poster <img> fine, but can leave the <video> itself rendering square
corners in Safari even though the exact same radius is already set two levels up. Setting the
same radius directly on the <video> element sidesteps that: its own corners are rounded
regardless of whether the ancestor's clip is honored. The lightbox-expanded state already does
this for itself further down (video-media.is-lightbox-expanded video) - this is the same fix for
the resting thumbnail, which that rule doesn't cover.
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ wrapper_id }} video-media > video {
  border-radius: {{ resolved_corner_radius }} !important;
}

{%- comment -%}
Videos only (media-zoom-slide.liquid adds .has-video-logo to video items; sections/header.liquid defines --video-logo
on :root only when the header has a transparent-header logo, so without one nothing is drawn): that logo, small, bottom-left, at 70% opacity. A pseudo-element of the video-media itself,
so it stays on the video wherever it goes (thumbnail, zoom animation, lightbox, vertical feed).
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ wrapper_id }} .has-video-logo video-media.is-lightbox-expanded::after {
  inset-block-end: 1.75rem;
  width: min(5rem, 25%);
  height: 2.75rem;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} .has-video-logo video-media::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset-inline-start: 0.75rem;
  inset-block-end: 0.75rem;
  z-index: 2;
  width: min(3.5rem, 25%);
  height: 2rem;
  background: var(--video-logo) left bottom / contain no-repeat;
  opacity: .7;
  pointer-events: none;
}

{%- comment -%}
Photos (the Instagram feed's .instagram-feed__image-logo, an element of its own because an <img> cannot have a pseudo-element): the
same logo, in the same corner and with the same size as a video's, once the photo is expanded. It is pinned to the photo's own
bottom-left corner the way the video buttons are pinned to the bottom-right one: the box is the biggest one of the photo's ratio that
fits the lightbox area (same --lightbox-ratio the photo is sized with), and it fades with the backdrop like those buttons.
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ wrapper_id }} .content-box__background-media > .instagram-feed__image-logo.is-lightbox-expanded {
  --lightbox-box-w: min(var(--lightbox-media-w), calc(var(--lightbox-media-h) * var(--lightbox-ratio, 1)));
  --lightbox-box-h: calc(var(--lightbox-box-w) / var(--lightbox-ratio, 1));
  position: fixed;
  z-index: 999;
  inset-block: 50% auto;
  inset-inline: 50% auto;
  width: min(5rem, 25%);
  height: 2.75rem;
  translate: calc(var(--lightbox-box-w) / -2 + 0.75rem) calc(var(--lightbox-box-h) / 2 - 100% - 1.75rem);
  transition: opacity .2s ease;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} .content-box__background-media > .instagram-feed__image-logo.is-lightbox-expanded:not(.is-lightbox-visible) {
  opacity: 0 !important;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} .content-box__background-media > .instagram-feed__image-logo.is-lightbox-expanded.is-lightbox-visible {
  opacity: .7;
  transition-delay: .3s;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} video-media > img {
  z-index: 1;
  transition: opacity .3s ease;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} video-media > img.carousel-video-poster-hidden {
  opacity: 0 !important;
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* If autoplay is blocked by the browser, the shared video-media component falls back to the native
   play button (sets [suspended] + controls) so playback can still be started manually. Our poster
   image would otherwise sit on top of it and hide/block taps. */
#{{ wrapper_id }} video-media[suspended] > img {
  opacity: 0 !important;
  pointer-events: none;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-tap-to-play {
  cursor: pointer;
}

/* Root cause of controls inheriting Color Scheme colors: .circle-button (theme-wide, assets/theme.css)
   paints a scheme-derived tint via its own ::before pseudo-element on top of whatever background we
   set, and shifts icon `color` on hover via a separate scheme-derived color-mix(). Both are killed
   below for our buttons specifically, the same way the theme already opts individual variants out via
   ".circle-button--outline:before { content: none; }". From here on, background/border/icon color
   come exclusively from the --control-button-* variables (Global > Controls). */
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-sound-toggle:before,
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-pause-toggle:before,
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-product-link:before {
  content: none;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-sound-toggle,
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-pause-toggle,
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-product-link {
  transition: opacity 0.2s ease, background-color 0.2s ease, border-color 0.2s ease, color 0.2s ease;
}

/* Base position (top-right, matches "top" layout exactly). Declared before the device/layout rules
   below on purpose: the "center" override further down has the same specificity (#id .class), so it
   only wins because it comes later in the cascade. */
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-controls {
  width: auto;
  height: auto;
  position: absolute;
  inset-block-start: 1rem;
  inset-inline-end: 1rem;
  z-index: 1;
}

/* Always visible, no hover/tap-to-reveal, no auto-hide: nothing autoplays, so there's no playing
   video to hide these behind - the visitor needs to see the play button right away to know how to
   start it. */
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-sound-toggle,
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-pause-toggle,
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-product-link {
  opacity: 1;
  pointer-events: auto;
}

/* Opt-in via media-zoom-slide.liquid's reveal_controls_on_tap: true (toggled in
   media-zoom-script.liquid) - the opposite of the always-visible rule above, for an autoplaying
   video where the controls would otherwise permanently sit on top of it. visibility (not just
   opacity) is what actually disables the buttons while hidden: it's inherited by the buttons
   underneath regardless of their own opacity: 1/pointer-events: auto set above, which a plain
   pointer-events: none on this wrapper alone would NOT override (each descendant's own
   pointer-events is evaluated independently of an ancestor's). */
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-controls--tap-to-reveal {
  visibility: hidden;
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity .2s ease, visibility .2s ease;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-controls--tap-to-reveal.is-controls-revealed {
  visibility: visible;
  opacity: 1;
}

{%- if resolved_controls_position == 'progress_bar' -%}
  {%- if resolved_desktop_controls_position == 'center' -%}
    @media screen and (pointer: fine) {
      #{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-controls {
        inset-block-start: 50%;
        inset-inline-end: auto;
        inset-inline-start: 50%;
        transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
      }
    }
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
  Desktop never moves controls into the slot, so the slot is always empty here - hide it outright so
  it stops taking up gap/space and the progress bar's own flex-grow fills 100% of the row.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  @media screen and (pointer: fine) {
    #{{ controls_slot_id }} {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  {%- comment -%}
  Mobile is the only device that relocates the controls group into the slot, so it is the only one
  that needs to hide it at its original, overlaid-on-video position and only show it once it is an
  actual descendant of the slot.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  @media screen and (pointer: coarse) {
    #{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-controls {
      display: none;
    }

    #{{ controls_slot_id }} .carousel-video-controls {
      display: flex;
      position: static;
      inset: auto;
    }
  }
{%- endif -%}

@media screen and (pointer: fine) {
  #{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-pause-toggle:not([disabled]):hover,
  #{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-sound-toggle:not([disabled]):hover,
  #{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-product-link:not([disabled]):hover {
    background: var(--control-button-hover-background-color, var(--control-button-background-color, #333333));
    color: var(--control-button-icon-color, #ffffff);
  }
}

/* Text mode reuses the theme's body text styling as-is (no independent typography). */
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-product-link__text {
  font: var(--text-font-style) var(--text-font-weight) var(--text-base) / var(--text-line-height) var(--text-font-family);
  text-transform: var(--text-transform);
  letter-spacing: var(--text-letter-spacing);
  white-space: nowrap;
}

{%- if resolved_controls_position == 'progress_bar' and has_multiple_slides -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    The product link stays hidden in this layout on both devices. Controls sit on the left, progress
    bar fills the rest on the right, on mobile - same two flex children as always, just reordered
    visually via the standard flexbox `order` property, not duplicated markup.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  #{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-product-link {
    display: none;
  }

  #{{ wrapper_id }} .progress-bar.grow {
    order: 2;
  }

  #{{ controls_slot_id }} {
    order: 1;
  }
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
This lightbox never moves the video/photo element into itself - it only toggles a class on the SAME
element already sitting in its slide. Moving a live, playing <video> across parents kept surfacing new
problems: the browser re-buffering it, video-media's own [loaded]/[playing] attributes going stale, and
its built-in one-time "click to play" listener firing a second time and colliding with the move.

#{{ lightbox_id }} itself is only used to hide/show the backdrop and the close/prev/next buttons
together via one [hidden] toggle - it carries no styling of its own beyond the theme's own .contents
utility (class="contents"), which is exactly what keeps it a zero-height grid item: every current
child (backdrop, close, prev, next) is position: fixed and so never becomes a real grid item once
hoisted. display: contents also means it can never gain a stacking context of its own the way
position: fixed would - if it did, the close/prev/next buttons' z-index would only be compared
against their siblings INSIDE that context, never able to outrank .is-lightbox-expanded media, which
lives outside it entirely.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- comment -%}
The backdrop behind the lightbox is the drawers' own: the same color and intensity (Theme settings > Drawers and popups > Backdrop
color / intensity: --page-overlay) and the same blur (--dialog-backdrop-blur), on every screen size. It no longer has a background
of its own. The buttons keep their own look. (The centered mobile drawers use a separate, fully opaque overlay color of their own,
--drawer-mobile-overlay-color, which is deliberately not used here: on an opaque color the blur would not show.)
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__backdrop {
  background: rgb(var(--page-overlay));
  -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(var(--dialog-backdrop-blur));
  backdrop-filter: blur(var(--dialog-backdrop-blur));
  z-index: 998;
  position: fixed;
  inset: 0;
  opacity: 1;
  transition: opacity .48s cubic-bezier(0.42, 0, 0.2, 1);
}

{%- comment -%}
Open/close animation: snippets/media-zoom-script.liquid removes [hidden] first (so the backdrop/
buttons/media actually paint), then adds "is-lightbox-visible" one frame later so this rule's opacity
change is a transition instead of an instant jump - and does the reverse on close, only hiding for
real once the transition has finished. "is-lightbox-visible" is added to #{{ lightbox_id }} itself
(reaching the backdrop/close/prev/next below via this descendant selector) AND, separately, directly
onto whichever video-media/img/.carousel-video-controls is current (see further down) - those live
outside #{{ lightbox_id }} entirely, so they need the class on themselves to be reachable here.
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ lightbox_id }}:not(.is-lightbox-visible) .media-lightbox__backdrop {
  opacity: 0;
}

{%- comment -%}
The expanded media has no fade/scale rule of its own: it always sits in its final, centered state, and
snippets/media-zoom-script.liquid animates it from/to the thumbnail's own box with a transform (the
open/close "zoom"), so it grows out of - and shrinks back into - the exact spot it was tapped in, instead
of disappearing from the grid and popping up (and back) somewhere else.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%}
.content-box > * (theme.css) sets isolation: isolate on .content-box__background-media, which traps
any z-index/position: fixed inside it to compete only within that local stacking context - it would
never be able to render above anything outside .content-box no matter how high its own z-index is.
Cancelling it here, only while something inside is expanded, lets the fixed media escape correctly.
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ wrapper_id }} .content-box__background-media:has(.is-lightbox-expanded) {
  isolation: auto;
}

{%- comment -%}
A `position: fixed` element is normally fixed to the viewport - UNLESS some ancestor between it and
the page root has a transform/perspective/filter/will-change/contain value other than none, in which
case it becomes fixed to THAT ancestor's box instead (even an inert `transform: translateY(0px)`
counts). FAQ's category tab panels (.faq__panel) are exactly that ancestor: they get a
`transform: translateY(0px)` entrance-animation style from their tab-switch JS, which otherwise traps
the expanded video/image inside the panel's own (scrolled, not full-height) box instead of the
screen - stripped here only while something inside is actually expanded.

Scoped to .faq__panel specifically rather than a bare `*:has(...)` on purpose: a universal selector
combined with :has() forces the browser to re-check every element on the page, not just this one
class, on every DOM change anywhere - including unrelated ones like the accordion's own open/close
height animation or the tab-switch animation itself, which visibly janked/"trembled" under that cost.
If a future caller of this snippet hits the same transform-trap problem with a different ancestor,
add its class here too rather than going back to `*`.
{%- endcomment -%}
.faq__panel:has(.is-lightbox-expanded) {
  transform: none !important;
}

{%- comment -%}
Drawer-style framing: the expanded media is centered with padding on every side (same tokens
.drawer::part(body) uses) instead of filling the screen edge to edge, sized to fit within that padded
area without cropping, and rounded to match the item's own corners.

Its size is never left to the browser ("auto" would size a video by whatever it has loaded so far and
let it grow once its metadata arrives): media-zoom-script.liquid sets --lightbox-ratio (the media's real
width / height) before the zoom starts, and the box is always the biggest one of that shape that fits the
area, so it is final from the first frame - the thumbnail-to-lightbox zoom lands on it exactly, every item
gets the same treatment, and nothing resizes afterwards.
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ wrapper_id }} {
  --lightbox-media-w: 96vw;
  --lightbox-media-h: 96vh;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} video-media.is-lightbox-expanded,
#{{ wrapper_id }} img.is-lightbox-expanded {
  position: fixed;
  z-index: 999;
  inset-block-start: 50%;
  inset-inline-start: 50%;
  translate: -50% -50%;
  width: min(var(--lightbox-media-w), calc(var(--lightbox-media-h) * var(--lightbox-ratio, 1)));
  height: auto;
  aspect-ratio: var(--lightbox-ratio, auto);
  max-width: none;
  max-height: none;
  border-radius: {{ resolved_corner_radius }};
  scale: 1;
  opacity: 1;
}

{%- comment -%}
--lightbox-media-w/h drive both the mobile media box AND the close/prev/next positions below, so
those stay pinned to its actual corners instead of the screen's. Height falls back to vh, then
upgrades to 100dvh where supported (same pattern as .modal--age-verifier in theme.css) so it's based
on the real visible area, not the layout viewport Chrome/Safari report before their address bar has
collapsed.
{%- endcomment -%}
@media screen and (max-width: 999px) {
  #{{ wrapper_id }} {
    --lightbox-media-w: min(calc(100vw - 2 * var(--container-gutter)), 32rem);
    --lightbox-media-h: calc(90vh - 2 * var(--container-gutter));
  }
}

@supports (height: 100dvh) {
  @media screen and (max-width: 999px) {
    #{{ wrapper_id }} {
      --lightbox-media-h: calc(90dvh - 2 * var(--container-gutter));
    }
  }
}

@media screen and (max-width: 999px) {
  #{{ wrapper_id }} video-media.is-lightbox-expanded {
    width: var(--lightbox-media-w);
    height: var(--lightbox-media-h);
  }

  #{{ wrapper_id }} video-media.is-lightbox-expanded video {
    border-radius: inherit;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} video-media.is-lightbox-expanded video {
  border-radius: inherit;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} img.is-lightbox-expanded {
  object-fit: contain;
}

#{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__close {
  background: var(--close-control-background-color, rgb(var(--button-background-primary)));
  border: 1px solid var(--close-control-border-color, transparent);
  color: var(--close-control-icon-color, rgb(var(--button-text-primary)));
  width: var(--control-button-size, 1.75rem);
  height: var(--control-button-size, 1.75rem);
  border-radius: 100%;
  z-index: 1000;
  place-content: center;
  display: grid;
  position: fixed;
  inset-block-start: 1rem;
  inset-inline-end: 1rem;
  opacity: 1;
  scale: 1;
  transition: opacity .2s ease, scale .2s ease;
}

{%- comment -%}
Overrides circle-button--xl's own --button-size (2.5rem/3rem) so close/prev/next all match the same
--control-button-size (Theme settings > Global > Controls) the play/pause/sound buttons already use.

The lightbox is a vertical feed (see the "Vertical feed" part of media-zoom-script.liquid): prev/next are
an up/down pair stacked at the right edge, centered vertically, like a short-video player's. They keep
the theme's left/right arrow icons, just turned a quarter turn (the "<" points up, the ">" down) - the
rotation is done here, with the individual `rotate` property, so the icon itself is untouched and
direction_aware's own RTL flip isn't involved.
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__prev,
#{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__next {
  width: var(--control-button-size, 1.75rem);
  height: var(--control-button-size, 1.75rem);
  z-index: 1000;
  position: fixed;
  inset-block-start: 50%;
  inset-inline-end: 1rem;
  opacity: 1;
  scale: 1;
  transition: opacity .2s ease, scale .2s ease;
}

#{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__prev {
  translate: 0 calc(-100% - 0.375rem);
}

#{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__next {
  translate: 0 0.375rem;
}

#{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__prev .icon,
#{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__next .icon {
  rotate: 90deg;
}

{%- comment -%}
First/last item: nothing further in that direction (the feed doesn't wrap around).
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__prev[disabled],
#{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__next[disabled] {
  opacity: .35;
  pointer-events: none;
}

#{{ lightbox_id }}:not(.is-lightbox-visible) .media-lightbox__close,
#{{ lightbox_id }}:not(.is-lightbox-visible) .media-lightbox__prev,
#{{ lightbox_id }}:not(.is-lightbox-visible) .media-lightbox__next {
  opacity: 0;
  scale: .8;
}

{%- comment -%}
Mobile only: pins close/prev/next to the media box's own right edge (using the same --lightbox-media-w/h
it's sized with) instead of the screen's. Not applied on desktop, where the media isn't a fixed-size
box so there's no fixed edge of its own to pin to.

Uses the button's full own width/height (100%), not half (50%), to offset - that lands the button's
edge flush with the anchor point instead of centering the button ON it, so the 0.75rem below reads as
an inward margin that keeps the whole button inside the media box's rounded corners.
{%- endcomment -%}
@media screen and (max-width: 999px) {
  #{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__close {
    inset-inline-end: auto;
    inset-inline-start: 50%;
    inset-block-start: 50%;
    translate: calc(var(--lightbox-media-w) / 2 - 100% - 0.75rem) calc(var(--lightbox-media-h) / -2 + 0.75rem);
  }

  #{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__prev,
  #{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__next {
    inset-inline-start: 50%;
    inset-inline-end: auto;
  }

  #{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__prev {
    translate: calc(var(--lightbox-media-w) / 2 - 100% - 0.75rem) calc(-100% - 0.375rem);
  }

  #{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__next {
    translate: calc(var(--lightbox-media-w) / 2 - 100% - 0.75rem) 0.375rem;
  }
}

{%- comment -%}
Playback scrubber (lightbox videos only, built by media-zoom-script.liquid as a child of <video-media>, so it
travels with the video). Runs edge to edge along the bottom of the video, with the logo (left) and the controls
(right) sitting just above it, and uses the controls' icon color. Hidden everywhere except in the lightbox.
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ wrapper_id }} .lightbox-scrubber {
  display: none;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} video-media.is-lightbox-expanded .lightbox-scrubber {
  display: block;
  position: absolute;
  z-index: 3;
  inset-inline: 0;
  inset-block-end: 0;
  height: 1.25rem;
  color: var(--control-button-icon-color, #ffffff);
  cursor: pointer;
  touch-action: none;
  filter: drop-shadow(0 0 2px rgb(0 0 0 / .45));
}

{%- comment -%}
A taller invisible touch area above the thin line, so it is easy to grab without making the line itself bigger.
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ wrapper_id }} .lightbox-scrubber::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: -0.75rem 0 0;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} .lightbox-scrubber__svg {
  display: block;
  overflow: visible;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} .lightbox-scrubber__rest,
#{{ wrapper_id }} .lightbox-scrubber__played {
  fill: none;
  stroke: currentColor;
  stroke-linecap: round;
  stroke-linejoin: round;
  stroke-width: var(--icon-stroke-width, 1.1px);
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} .lightbox-scrubber__rest {
  opacity: .35;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} .lightbox-scrubber__dot {
  fill: currentColor;
  transform-box: fill-box;
  transform-origin: center;
  animation: lightbox-scrubber-breathe 1.8s ease-in-out infinite;
  transition: scale .2s ease;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} .lightbox-scrubber.is-scrubbing .lightbox-scrubber__dot {
  scale: 1.5;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} .lightbox-scrubber__time {
  position: absolute;
  inset-block-end: 100%;
  translate: -50% 0;
  margin-block-end: 0.125rem;
  font: var(--text-font-style) var(--text-font-weight) 0.6875rem / 1 var(--text-font-family);
  letter-spacing: 0.02em;
  opacity: 0;
  pointer-events: none;
  transition: opacity .2s ease;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} .lightbox-scrubber.is-scrubbing .lightbox-scrubber__time {
  opacity: 1;
}

@keyframes lightbox-scrubber-breathe {
  0%, 100% { transform: scale(1); }
  50% { transform: scale(1.3); }
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  #{{ wrapper_id }} .lightbox-scrubber__dot {
    animation: none;
  }
}

{%- comment -%}
The feed is driven by vertical drags: the browser must not treat them as page scroll/zoom or select the
media, and the media is never draggable as a native image.
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__backdrop,
#{{ wrapper_id }} video-media.is-lightbox-expanded,
#{{ wrapper_id }} img.is-lightbox-expanded {
  touch-action: none;
  user-select: none;
  -webkit-user-select: none;
}

{%- comment -%}
product-zoom-button (theme.css) inherits currentColor and has a negative inset-inline-end meant to
slightly overhang a card's edge - here it sits inside .content-box's own overflow: hidden, so that
negative offset clips it off-center instead. Pinning both fixes it without touching the shared rule
product-gallery still uses as-is.
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ wrapper_id }} .product-zoom-button {
  color: #fff;
  inset-inline-end: 1rem;
}

{%- comment -%}
product-zoom-button__label (theme.css) starts at width: 0, only revealed on @media (pointer:
fine):hover - on touch devices that hover never fires, so it stays hidden forever. Showing it always
on mobile instead of on hover here.
{%- endcomment -%}
@media screen and (pointer: coarse) {
  #{{ wrapper_id }} .product-zoom-button__label {
    width: auto;
  }
}

{%- comment -%}
The video's own sound/pause toggles stay exactly where they are in the DOM (same as the video itself)
- this just switches them from "absolute, relative to their own .content-box" to "fixed, relative to
the viewport" so they land near the bottom-right of the expanded video instead of the small thumbnail.
display: flex overrides the touch + progress_bar layout's display: none (that rule expects this group
to only ever be visible once relocated into the controls slot, which doesn't apply here).
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-controls.is-lightbox-expanded {
  opacity: 1 !important;
  pointer-events: auto !important;
  display: flex !important;
  position: fixed !important;
  z-index: 999;
  inset-block-end: var(--drawer-body-padding-block, min(2rem, var(--container-gutter)));
  inset-inline-end: var(--drawer-body-padding-inline, min(2rem, var(--container-gutter)));
  inset-block-start: auto;
  flex-direction: column;
  transition: opacity .2s ease;
}

{%- comment -%}
Pinned to the media box's own bottom-right corner (same inset the video's logo has on the bottom-left, 1.75rem up so both clear the scrubber),
so the logo and these controls sit on one line. The box is computed from the same --lightbox-ratio
the media itself is sized with (media-zoom-script.liquid sets them on both). Mobile has its own fixed box, below.
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-controls.is-lightbox-expanded {
  --lightbox-box-w: min(var(--lightbox-media-w), calc(var(--lightbox-media-h) * var(--lightbox-ratio, 1)));
  --lightbox-box-h: calc(var(--lightbox-box-w) / var(--lightbox-ratio, 1));
  inset-block-end: auto;
  inset-inline-end: auto;
  inset-inline-start: 50%;
  inset-block-start: 50%;
  transform: none;
  translate: calc(var(--lightbox-box-w) / 2 - 100% - 0.75rem) calc(var(--lightbox-box-h) / 2 - 100% - 1.75rem);
}

{%- comment -%}
Fades with the backdrop and close/prev/next buttons instead of popping in beside the still-zooming
video on open, and sitting in the corner until the zoom ends on close:
media-zoom-script.liquid toggles is-lightbox-visible on this group together with the lightbox's.
{%- endcomment -%}
#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-controls.is-lightbox-expanded:not(.is-lightbox-visible) {
  opacity: 0 !important;
  pointer-events: none !important;
}

#{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-controls.is-lightbox-expanded.is-lightbox-visible {
  transition-delay: .3s;
}

{%- comment -%}
Mobile only: same corner-pinning as close/prev/next above, so this lands inside the video's own
bottom-right corner instead of the screen's.
{%- endcomment -%}
@media screen and (max-width: 999px) {
  #{{ wrapper_id }} .carousel-video-controls.is-lightbox-expanded {
    inset-block-end: auto;
    inset-inline-end: auto;
    inset-inline-start: 50%;
    inset-block-start: 50%;
    translate: calc(var(--lightbox-media-w) / 2 - 100% - 0.75rem) calc(var(--lightbox-media-h) / 2 - 100% - 1.75rem);
  }
}

{%- comment -%}
Respect "reduce motion": the open/close still happens (classes still toggle, snippets/
media-zoom-script.liquid still waits for a transitionend-or-timeout before actually hiding), it just
happens instantly instead of animated.
{%- endcomment -%}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  #{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__backdrop,
  #{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__close,
  #{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__prev,
  #{{ lightbox_id }} .media-lightbox__next {
    transition: none;
  }
}
